(CHAPTER I.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  _Ancient Mode of supporting Governments.?Ignorance of) Tj T*
(  Political Economy.?Mercantile Greatness.?Early Supplies.?Tulip) Tj T*
(  Mania.?Accession of William._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The national debt has been designated by some a national nuisance;) Tj T*
(by others it has been termed a national necessity. In the earlier) Tj T*
(history of the world, when war was a war for dominion, and spoliation) Tj T*
(followed conquest, the victor returned rich with the treasures of) Tj T*
(conquered states, and his captive paid trebly the expenses of the) Tj T*
(war. It was thus that the mistress of the world became an emporium) Tj T*
(for the gathered wealth of temples, for the gorgeous ornaments of a) Tj T*
(subdued aristocracy, and for the gold which had filled the treasury) Tj T*
(of barbarous but luxurious nations. These accumulations, together) Tj T*
(with annual tributes, prevented the formation of a public debt. The) Tj T*
(Goth, when he poured from his barren recesses upon the cultivated) Tj T*
(plains of Italy, ignorant of political economy as a science, felt it) Tj T*
(as a principle, and more than repaid the expenses of his foray by) Tj T*
(exacting the riches of imperial Rome. Modern Europe teaches us to) Tj T*
(similar purport; and Napoleon, in those wars which, to some a memory,) Tj T*
(are to others history, acted upon the same plan, and made Paris a) Tj T*
(receptacle for the spoil of many nations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the early annals of England, the feudal system prevented the) Tj T*
(creation of a national debt. The Saxon serf was compelled to follow) Tj T*
(the banner of his Norman master. The Norman baron, at the command) Tj T*
(of his sovereign, called his followers to the field, and having,) Tj T*
(if successful, enriched himself, retraced his path to his mountain) Tj T*
(fastness and his island home. In these rude ages the art of levying) Tj T*
(money was unknown; and victorious armies were often dispersed for) Tj T*
(want of funds. The conqueror of Pavia was compelled to disband 24,000) Tj T*
(men because he could not raise taxes to support them; and it is a) Tj T*
(suggestive fact, that when, during the reign of the third Henry, it) Tj T*
(was necessary to procure г50,000, and a tax of г1 2_s._ 4_d._ was) Tj T*
(levied on each parish in England, it only produced about г9,500,) Tj T*
(there being but 8,500 parishes; so ignorant were the authorities of) Tj T*
(the very machinery of the state they governed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From a very early period, the mercantile capacity of England has been) Tj T*
