(developed; and her insular position, which at once suggested and) Tj T*
(favored commerce, was taken advantage of by laymen and churchmen.) Tj T*
(Bishops entered into speculations in herrings, and abbots did not) Tj T*
(disdain to unite the smuggling with a more saintly calling. But) Tj T*
(there were other and more legitimate followers of that pursuit which) Tj T*
(has since made the name of an English merchant a symbol of English) Tj T*
(greatness. Among these, William de la Pole stands prominently) Tj T*
(forward; and the founder of the House of Suffolk is familiar to the) Tj T*
(student of commercial history. William Canyng?that name so intimately) Tj T*
(connected with the fortunes of ?the marvellous boy who perished in) Tj T*
(his pride??and Richard Whittington?dear to household memories, and) Tj T*
(the founder of many princely charities?were others whose munificence) Tj T*
(was only surpassed by their wealth.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A slight sketch of the tyranny and injustice employed by our earlier) Tj T*
(monarchs in the production of revenue, may not be unamusing to the) Tj T*
(readers of the present volume. The records of the Exchequer prove) Tj T*
(that barbaric acts were performed to obtain money; that justice was) Tj T*
(openly bought and sold; that the supreme judicature of the country) Tj T*
(could only be approached by bribes to the monarch. The county of) Tj T*
(Norfolk paid a large sum to Henry I. to secure fair dealing. Yarmouth) Tj T*
(paid heavily to prevent a king from violating his own charter.) Tj T*
(Commerce was controlled, and trade was harassed. Corporations and) Tj T*
(monopolies were created at the monarch?s pleasure; and, as nothing) Tj T*
(was too small to escape his notice, so nothing was too large to) Tj T*
(escape his grasp. The wife of Hugh de Neville paid two hundred hens) Tj T*
(to enjoy the society of her husband twelve hours in prison; and an) Tj T*
(abbot paid largely for permission to secure his wood from being) Tj T*
(stolen. To mitigate the king?s anger, or obtain the king?s services,) Tj T*
(money was equally necessary. When peer and prior were sufficiently) Tj T*
(strong to resist, or sufficiently poor to escape, the farmer and the) Tj T*
(peasant were visited. The approach of the court was like the approach) Tj T*
(of the plague; and men ran to conceal their effects and their persons) Tj T*
(until the royal plunderer had passed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Extraordinary emergencies caused extraordinary expenses; and the) Tj T*
(call to arms which resounded throughout Europe when Peter the Hermit) Tj T*
(preached deliverance to the captive Sepulchre, was responded to by) Tj T*
(Richard I. with the vehemence and energy of his character. To compass) Tj T*
(his aim, he mortgaged the customs and he farmed the revenues. He) Tj T*
(exacted money from his subjects in proportion to their wealth, and) Tj T*
(declared he would sell London itself rather than forego his cherished) Tj T*
(object. He feigned the loss of his signet, to procure fees; and, to) Tj T*
(crown all, resumed, on his return, the property he had previously) Tj T*
